---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import FormingSim from '../components/islands/FormingSim.astro';
import vis from '../data/viscosity.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
// Furnace to softening point on the NBS 710 Fulcher fit, so the ratio in the text follows the data.
const { A, B, T0 } = vis.fulcher;
const furnace = vis.studio.find((s) => s.id === 'furnace')!.T;
const soft = vis.points.find((p) => p.id === 'softening')!;
const logFurnace = A + B / (furnace - T0) - 1; // log10 Pa·s
const ratio = 10 ** (soft.logPas - logFurnace);
const fold = `${Math.round(ratio / 1000) * 1000}`.replace(/\B(?=(\d{3})+$)/, ',');
---
<Base title="Forming Lab" description="A gather of hot glass on a blowpipe: viscosity from a measured soda-lime glass, sag, blowing and swinging from viscous flow, cooling by radiation in three bands.">
  <article class="concept">
    <h1>Forming lab</h1>
    <p class="summary">
      Glassblowing is a race against viscosity. A gather fresh from the furnace is runny enough to drip
      off the pipe in a couple of seconds; a minute later its skin is past the working point. Everything a
      blower does, turning, reheating, blowing in puffs, swinging, is a way of steering three slow flows
      while the glass cools. Here they are, computed.
    </p>
    <FormingSim />

    <h2>Things to try</h2>
    <p>
      <b>Stop turning.</b> Set the turn to zero and the gather sags off the pipe within a few seconds.
      Turn it again and look at the end-on view: the far end no longer falls; it circles a point off to
      one side. Turning does not stop the glass from flowing. It reverses the direction of the flow
      every half turn, so the sag cancels itself instead of adding up.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Blow and hold.</b> The first bubble is the hard part. At the tip of the pipe it is a few
      millimetres across, and the glass around a small bubble must stretch hard to let it grow, so a
      gather that has cooled for half a minute hardly moves. Once it starts, it runs away: a thinner wall
      is easier to stretch, so the same breath stretches it faster, until your breath rather than the
      glass sets the pace. Keep holding and the wall thins until it chills and sets as a thin, oversized
      bubble. Blowers start the bubble while the gather is hot, then blow in short puffs with reheats
      between, so the wall never gets ahead of them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Swing.</b> Point the pipe down and the gather stretches under its own weight; point it up and it
      settles back, shorter and fatter. Swinging the pipe is how a blower makes length.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Reheat.</b> In the glory hole a gather takes heat back by the same bands it loses heat by. Watch
      the clear one: near the chamber's own temperature it all but disappears against the walls, the
      same law that makes it glow dimly in the dark shop (see <a href={`${base}/#heat`}>the glory hole</a>).
    </p>

    <h2>The three flows</h2>
    <p>
      Viscosity sets the pace of every flow, and for soda-lime glass it changes about {fold}-fold between
      the furnace at a typical {furnace} °C (about {Math.round(10 ** logFurnace / 10) * 10} Pa·s on this curve; the furnace figure is unverified) and the softening point
      at {soft.measured} °C, where a hanging fibre stretches under its own weight. The simulator takes it from one measured glass,
      NBS Standard Glass No. 710, through the Fulcher fit Napolitano and Hawkins made for it in 1964,
      the same curve as the site's <a href={`${base}/#glass`}>stage 00</a>. The gather is cut into 24 shells
      of equal mass, each with its own temperature and so its own viscosity, and each flow is the
      textbook result for a viscous body of that shape.
    </p>
    <h3>Blowing: a hollow sphere</h3>
    <p>
      Glass is incompressible, so when the bubble grows every shell must move outward with velocity
      u = C/r², whatever its temperature. That fixes the flow, and the pressure it needs follows
      exactly: Δp = ∫ 12 η(r) C / r⁴ dr across the wall. Two consequences fall out. The 1/r⁴ weight puts
      almost all the stretching in the glass next to the bubble, so in a thick gather the core's heat,
      not the skin's, decides whether it will blow. And for a thin wall the rate becomes
      da/dt = Δp a² / 12 η h: it grows as the bubble grows and the wall thins, which is the runaway.
      Surface tension on both faces pulls back by 2γ/r each, which matters only for the smallest bubbles.
    </p>
    <h3>Sagging: a viscous cantilever</h3>
    <p>
      The part of the gather beyond the pipe's tip is a short beam under its own weight. Slow viscous
      flow obeys the same equations as
      elastic deformation, with velocities in place of displacements and 3η in place of Young's modulus
      (the Stokes–Rayleigh analogy, exact for incompressible material, after Stokes in 1845 and Rayleigh's <i>Theory of Sound</i>, as Teichman and Mahadevan attribute it). So the elastic tip deflection wL⁴/8EI becomes a
      deflection <i>rate</i>. Its stiffness weights each shell by r³, the opposite of blowing: the skin
      carries the bending. That is the blower's trick, as the model has it: let the skin chill and it
      holds the gather straight while the core is still hot enough to blow.
    </p>
    <p>
      Turning the pipe at ω carries the sag round with the glass. In the shop's frame the far end obeys
      d′ = ω × d + v ĝ, and the solution circles the point v/ω to one side of the pipe with radius v/ω.
      For a fresh gather turned once a second, the far end strays less than a centimetre; stopped, the
      same glass falls two or three centimetres a second (both from the model).
    </p>
    <h3>Swinging: stretching under weight</h3>
    <p>
      Along the pipe, the weight of the glass pulls the gather into a longer spheroid at a strain rate
      σ/3η, where 3η is the extensional viscosity Frederick Trouton measured for pitch and other very
      viscous liquids in 1906.
    </p>

    <h2>Cooling, in three bands</h2>
    <p>
      Out of the fire the gather loses heat mainly as radiation, and glass is not a simple radiator.
      Liu, Kitamura, Xia and Pilon measured soda-lime melts in 2016 and described their absorption in
      two bands. Below 2.8 µm the glass is nearly transparent: clear glass absorbs about 43 per metre
      there, so light crosses centimetres of it, while green bottle glass, with six times the iron,
      absorbs about 179 per metre. From 2.8 to 5 µm every soda-lime glass absorbs about 460 per metre,
      and beyond 5 µm it is opaque. At 1100 °C a black body sends about 45% of its power into the first
      band, 35% into the second and 20% into the opaque one (computed from Planck's law).
    </p>
    <p>
      So each band leaves from a different depth. The opaque band leaves from the skin, the second from
      the outer few millimetres, and the first from deep inside a clear gather. Each shell here emits each
      band in proportion to how much of it can reach the surface, by the exact result for a non-scattering
      slab (the kernel 2κE₂(κd), whose total is the slab's emissivity, 1 − 2E₃(κh)). Two things follow
      that the simulator shows. A thin blown wall is a poorer emitter than the solid gather, because thin
      glass is transparent to its own first band, the point Robert Gardon made in 1956. And it cools much
      faster anyway, because the same glass now has three or four times the surface. Heat moves between
      shells by conduction, with the conductivity Liu and colleagues measured.
    </p>
    <p>
      The glow is computed the way <a href={`${base}/#heat`}>the glory hole</a> computes it: Planck's law
      times the glass's own visible emissivity, one minus its transmission along a line of sight through
      the wall (Kirchhoff, 1860). Each shell is drawn at its own temperature, so a chilled skin shows as a
      dimmer rim around a brighter core.
    </p>

    <h2>What the model leaves out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The gather is an equal-volume sphere for heat and blowing, and a spheroid only for drawing,
        sagging and stretching. Real gathers are lopsided, and blowers use uneven heat to shape them;
        here every shell is the same all the way round.</li>
      <li>The beam formula is for slender beams; a gather is stubby, so the sag rate is an order of
        magnitude, not a prediction. The free length is taken as the far half of the gather.</li>
      <li>Radiation the wall trades with itself is ignored, the sphere is treated as a slab for emission,
        and the glory hole heats by radiation alone (no flame). Absorption is the room-temperature
        two-band data; colorants' own infrared absorption is ignored, so a cobalt gather cools like a
        clear one here.</li>
      <li>No marver, jacks or paddles (contact cooling and shaping), no capping the pipe to let trapped air
        blow the bubble, no thermal shock or annealing stress (for that, the lehr).</li>
      <li>Heat capacity, surface tension, convection, breath pressure and the most a blower can exhale are
        assumed typical values, listed in <code>src/data/forming.json</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Viscosity: A. Napolitano and E. G. Hawkins, "Viscosity of a standard soda-lime-silica glass",
        <i>J. Res. NBS</i> 68A (1964) 439, <a href="https://doi.org/10.6028/jres.068A.042">doi:10.6028/jres.068A.042</a>.</li>
      <li>Conductivity and absorption bands: H. Liu, R. Kitamura, X. Xia and L. Pilon, "Conductive and
        radiative properties of soda-lime silicate glassmelts with different iron contents from 1100 °C to
        1500 °C", <i>J. Am. Ceram. Soc.</i> 99 (2016) 1271, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/jace.14095">doi:10.1111/jace.14095</a>.</li>
      <li>Emissivity of thin glass: R. Gardon, "The emissivity of transparent materials", <i>J. Am. Ceram.
        Soc.</i> 39 (1956) 278.</li>
      <li>Extensional viscosity: F. T. Trouton, "On the coefficient of viscous traction and its relation to
        that of viscosity", <i>Proc. R. Soc. Lond. A</i> 77 (1906) 426–440, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1906.0038">doi:10.1098/rspa.1906.0038</a>.</li>
      <li>Elastic–viscous analogy: J. Teichman and L. Mahadevan, "The viscous catenary", <i>J. Fluid Mech.</i> (2003),
        <a href="https://softmath.seas.harvard.edu/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/2003-02.pdf">PDF</a>, which credits it to G. G. Stokes,
        <i>Trans. Camb. Phil. Soc.</i> 8 (1845) 287, and Lord Rayleigh, <i>The Theory of Sound</i>, vol. II.</li>
      <li>Kirchhoff's law: G. Kirchhoff, <i>Annalen der Physik</i> 109 (1860) 275.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="back"><a href={`${base}/#heat`}>← back to the glory hole</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>
